    h1: "How Is HVAC Engineered for Indoor Vertical Farm Facilities?",
    answer: "The engineering answer is that indoor farm HVAC is designed around water, not just temperature. Plants transpire most of the irrigation water they receive, and every drop that leaves the leaves becomes latent load the HVAC system must remove. Direct answer: the engineer sizes dehumidification capacity to the crop's peak transpiration rate, adds the sensible heat of the LED lighting, and designs air distribution that holds tight temperature and humidity bands so the crop stays in its target vapor pressure deficit range.\n\nLatent load dominates the calculation in a way that surprises owners coming from conventional commercial buildings. In an office, people and ventilation drive the latent load; in a sealed grow room, the canopy is effectively a giant evaporator. The engineer starts from the irrigation plan and the crop's water use, converts it to moisture removal required per hour at peak transpiration, and selects dehumidification equipment — dedicated dehumidifiers, desiccant systems, or HVAC with reheat — that can hold relative humidity through lights-on peaks without overcooling the room. Undersize this and the room drifts into disease-friendly humidity; oversize it and the energy bill eats the crop margin.\n\nSensible load comes mostly from the lights. LED fixtures convert a large share of their electrical input to heat released directly into the grow space, so the cooling load tracks the lighting power almost one for one. The engineer calculates watts per square foot of canopy across every tier, applies diversity for photoperiod staging, and sizes cooling to hold the target temperature even when all zones run at full output. Air distribution matters as much as capacity: air must move uniformly through and around the racks so no canopy pocket stagnates, since still air invites mold and uneven growth. Ductwork or fan layouts are drawn to push air horizontally across each tier rather than dumping it from the ceiling.\n\nControls tie the whole system to the crop rather than to a thermostat. The sequence targets vapor pressure deficit — the combined temperature-humidity metric growers actually manage — instead of holding temperature and humidity as independent setpoints. Sensors sit at canopy level in multiple zones, not on a wall by the door, and the controls stage cooling, dehumidification, and reheat against each other without fighting. Redundancy is a business decision the engineer prices honestly: a climate failure in a sealed farm can destroy a crop in hours, so critical rooms often get N+1 dehumidification or backup cooling. Where the project is in California, the design must satisfy the 2025 California Energy Code / 2025 Standards, effective January 1, 2026, which pushes the engineer toward high-efficiency dehumidification and documented controls sequences.",
