    slug: "vertical-farm-lighting-design",
    title: "What Does Vertical Farm Lighting Design Involve for Growers?",
    description: "Vertical farm lighting design sets PPFD, spectrum, and photoperiod per crop tier, balancing daily light integral targets against energy use and HVAC heat loads.",
    h1: "What Does Vertical Farm Lighting Design Involve for Growers?",
    answer: "The engineering answer is that vertical farm lighting design is a crop-production calculation expressed in electrical and mechanical terms. Every tier of the rack needs a defined amount of photosynthetic light delivered uniformly to the canopy, on a schedule the crop expects, without cooking the plants or bankrupting the operation on power. Direct answer: the engineer sets a target daily light integral per crop, selects LED fixtures for spectrum and efficacy, lays out mounting heights and spacing for canopy uniformity, and coordinates the electrical load and the heat with the building systems.\n\nThe design starts with the crop's light requirement, expressed as PPFD — photosynthetic photon flux density, the intensity of grow-usable light hitting the canopy — and as DLI, the daily light integral, which is PPFD accumulated over the photoperiod. Leafy greens typically target moderate PPFD over long photoperiods, while fruiting crops want higher intensity; the engineer converts the grower's DLI target into fixture counts per tier using the fixture's published photon output and the mounting height. Uniformity is designed, not hoped for: fixtures are spaced and aimed so the dimmest canopy point still lands within the acceptable band, because uneven light means uneven growth and uneven harvest.\n\nSpectrum selection is a horticultural decision the engineer implements. Modern LED grow fixtures offer tunable or fixed spectra weighted toward the red and blue bands plants use most efficiently, with the balance shifting by growth stage — vegetative growth favoring blue-heavy recipes, flowering and fruiting favoring red. The engineer verifies the fixture's spectrum against the crop plan, checks that dimming is smooth and flicker-free across the range the recipe needs, and confirms the photoperiod schedule the controls will run, since day-length response drives flowering in many crops.\n\nEvery lighting decision lands on the electrical and mechanical drawings. The connected load per tier sums to a service size that often surprises first-time developers, and the heat — most of the input wattage — becomes the HVAC cooling load. The engineer coordinates fixture mounting with the racking structure, routes power so each tier or zone can be controlled independently, and writes the lighting control sequence: photoperiod timing, dimming curves, and staging that ramps zones rather than slamming the electrical service with inrush. Where the project is in California, the lighting power and controls must satisfy the 2025 California Energy Code / 2025 Standards, effective January 1, 2026.",
