        heading: "Dosing, EC/pH Monitoring, and Mixing",
        body: "The control loop is only as good as its sensors. The engineer specifies industrial-grade EC and pH probes — not hobby meters — mounted in a properly mixed sample stream with isolation valves so probes can be removed for calibration without draining the system. Dosing pumps are sized with enough turndown to meter accurately across the full operating range, and the controller is programmed with dosing limits and alarms: if pH drifts beyond the correction band or EC climbs unexpectedly, the system alerts the grower instead of blindly dosing. Redundant probes on critical loops let the controller vote out a failed sensor rather than chasing a false reading.\n\nStock solution handling is designed for safety and accuracy. Concentrated fertilizers are corrosive and must be stored in compatible tanks with secondary containment, labeled per hazard requirements, and located where spills cannot reach drains or the crop. The engineer sizes stock tanks for a practical refill cadence — large enough that staff are not mixing daily, small enough that concentrates stay fresh — and details the mixing station with adequate ventilation, eyewash access, and clear labeling. Batch mixing tanks get agitators or recirculation so concentrates blend fully before the solution reaches the crop, because stratified tanks deliver inconsistent nutrition row to row.",
