        question: "How do demand charges change the lighting schedule?",
        answer: "They punish peaks. Many commercial tariffs bill a demand charge on the highest fifteen-minute usage each month, so a farm that starts every zone's lights simultaneously can set a costly peak in one instant. The engineer staggers zone startups, shifts photoperiods toward off-peak rate windows where the crop allows, and uses dimming to cap demand during peak periods — each strategy modeled against the actual tariff before the schedule is set.",
