        body: "Electrical is typically the largest capital item. The engineer sizes the ultimate service from the full-build lighting load plus HVAC, dehumidification, and pumps, then designs the phase-one installation — service entrance, main distribution, and the first grow rooms — so expansion means adding feeders, not replacing the service. Utility coordination starts during due diligence: the transformer upgrade, the service lateral, and the schedule the utility quotes become project constraints the phasing respects. Zoned distribution with independent lighting controls follows the grow-room plan from day one.\n\nClimate and plumbing upgrades follow the same phased logic. The engineer designs the HVAC and dehumidification for each phase's actual loads — lighting heat and transpiration at that phase's canopy — while routing mains and leaving capacity for what comes next. Plumbing brings filtered water with backflow protection, nutrient and condensate drainage to approved discharge, and the floor drainage production areas need; the underground work is done once, sized for the ultimate facility. Throughout, the engineer keeps the phase-one facility independently complete and permittable: each phase must grow, harvest, and pack on its own, because funding and schedules change and a half-built dependency helps no one.",
