        question: "What is biofilm and why does loop detailing matter?",
        answer: "Biofilm is the slimy microbial layer that colonizes wet surfaces — and in a recirculating loop it harbors pathogens, clogs emitters, and shields organisms from sterilization. It establishes wherever water stagnates or surfaces offer crevices, which is why the engineer eliminates dead legs, maintains circulation everywhere, specifies smooth joint methods, and designs scouring flushes. Detailing is the difference between a loop that stays clean and one that cultures problems.",
